#!/usr/bin/env etcs

# THE 3D SCENE, IN A BROWSER -- the same four things the OS-side script stands
# up, reached through the same verbs.
#
#     cd modules/RenderProvider/scripts && etcs serve_scene3d.etcs
#     then open https://localhost:8443/
#
# and with the pointer over the canvas: move it to look around. From the
# terminal beside it, while it is running:
#
#     Root> RenderProvider                              # read the RIDs back
#     Root> eye.LookAt(0.0, 12.0, -6.0, 0.0, 0.0, 6.0)  # move the eye
#     Root> tower_far.SetColor(1.0, 0.2, 0.2, 1.0)      # repaint one box
#     Root> block.SetVisible(0)                         # hide the occluder
#     Root> world.DepthAt(@eye, 512, 384)               # how far is the centre
#     Root> world.Order()                               # energy, heat, ticks
#
# WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. Everything the browser runtime had been shown to do was
# flat: a paint document, a toolbar, a colour wheel. All of that reaches the
# screen through Surface_ and Pixels_, so it exercises exactly the half of
# RenderProvider that a 2D page needs. The projection, the depth buffer, the
# camera-as-a-2D-node arrangement and the scene's own motion integration had
# never been run here at all -- so whether they worked was an assumption.
#
# THE DIFFERENCES FROM scripts/scene3d.etcs. Three are about the SUBSTRATE:
#
#   no main.Run()          the browser's event loop is the page's; the last
#                          stream edge blocks this worker instead
#   no CaptureMouse        pointer lock needs a user gesture in a browser, and
#                          an absolute look has nothing to confine anyway -- the
#                          pointer's position over the frame IS the angle
#   no profile script      that one exists to tear the window down on exit, and
#                          a tab closing is not an exit this runtime sees
#
# THE FOURTH IS AN OPEN BUG, AND IT IS WHY THE EDGES ARE ARRANGED AS THEY ARE
# BELOW -- not a browser limitation and not a preference. A projection running
# on a DETACHED thread corrupts the heap here: the next free faults, wherever it
# happens to be. Bisected, in this page, with everything else held constant:
#
#   2D tree, frame edge detached ........... stable (this is the paint page)
#   camera + scene, frame edge on THIS thread .... stable, and it renders
#   camera + scene, frame edge DETACHED .......... faults in free(), inside
#                                                 Scene3D::ProjectConcrete or
#                                                 CompositeDrawable2D::recompose
#   ... plus a key edge (ProduceEvents -> ConsumeInput)
#       detached ................................. faults in the loader, through
#                                                 checkMailbox -- a proxied call
#
# The projection code itself is fine: the same scene, the same camera and the
# same geometry draw correctly when the frame edge is stated on this thread, and
# the pixels were checked (sky, ground slab and three tower shades all distinct).
# So what is broken is a 3D projection's interaction with a detached thread, and
# until that is fixed this page states the frame edge HERE and detaches only the
# pointer -- which is measurably stable and leaves the look control working.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS: W/A/S/D. The key edge is also the window's event pump, so it
# wants this thread too, and only one edge can have it. The line is left below,
# commented, with this note rather than quietly omitted.
#
# THE STRUCTURE, four things, each doing one job:
#
#   world  a Scene3D  -- geometry, and the thing W/A/S/D translates
#   eye    a Camera3D -- the image plane; a Drawable2D that a scene fills
#   stage  a compositor -- where the 3D view and any 2D UI merge
#   view   a Surface  -- what the page's canvas presents
#
# and the seam between them is one call each way: the camera names the scene by
# RID, the compositor holds the camera as an ordinary 2D child, and the surface
# is handed the compositor as the root to walk. Nothing anywhere says "this one
# is the 3D one".

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

# Created before any edge is stated: the Surface takes its handle and extent
# from a live window.
spawn WindowProvider::Window main
main.Create(1024, 768, ETCS 3D scene)

main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu)

# ── the 2D side: a compositor, so UI and the 3D view merge in one buffer ─────
spawn RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D stage
stage.Create(1024, 768)
stage.SetPosition(0, 0)
stage.SetBackground(0.04, 0.05, 0.08, 1.0)

# ── the camera: a Drawable2D that a scene fills ──────────────────────────────
#
# Nested in the stage like any other 2D node, positioned in the stage's space
# like any other 2D node, ordered against its siblings like any other 2D node.
# It is a camera only in what fills it -- which is why the compositor, the
# published-frame path and the present edge need no case for it.
stage.spawn(RenderProvider::Camera3D eye)
eye.Create(1024, 768)
eye.SetPosition(0, 0)
eye.SetOrder(1)
eye.SetBackground(0.07, 0.09, 0.15, 1.0)
eye.LookAt(0.0, 3.0, -14.0, 0.0, 0.5, 2.0)
eye.SetLens(60.0, 0.1, 400.0)

# ── the scene: an invisible root, and the geometry under it ──────────────────
#
# The root is a container rather than a box you can see: it is the coordinate
# origin the scene is stated against and the single thing W/A/S/D translates.
spawn RenderProvider::Scene3D world
world.Create(1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
world.SetVisible(0)
world.SetPosition(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)

# Terminal speed and drag, per second -- held keys are an acceleration, not a
# displacement, so these two are the whole feel.
world.SetSpeed(7.0)
world.SetDamping(6.0)

# The yaw span: 2.0 makes the canvas's width two full turns, so a half turn
# lands a quarter of the way out from centre and the pointer stays near the
# middle of the frame. Pitch always maps top edge to bottom edge once. Nothing
# accumulates -- put the pointer back and the view is back.
world.SetSensitivity(2.0)

# The rate at which the scene's accumulated drag heat stops being the scene's
# problem; read the ledger back with world.Order().
world.SetEmissivity(0.5)

# The geometry itself, from the module's own reusable scene -- a ground slab,
# three towers receding, and a near block that occludes one of them without
# being related to it in the tree. Everything in it is stated in the anchor's
# space, so moving `world` moves the lot.
run RenderProvider/scripts/scene3d.etcs anchor=world

# One call, and the direction of causality: the camera names the scene.
eye.SetScene(@world)

# ── 2D UI, drawn ON the camera's frame ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# A child of the camera, in the camera's own space, drawn after the projection
# and clipped to the frame. This is the composition the Camera family exists
# for, and it is also the cheapest check that the two halves compose: if the
# caption is legible over the towers, a 2D node drew onto a 3D image.
#
# STATIC TEXT, DELIBERATELY. The OS-side script binds this label to the
# surface's frame rate with BindFps, which is worth having and is not free: a
# label whose text changes every frame answers yes to Animating(), which keeps
# every compositor above it recomposing forever. That is the one thing a page
# meant to demonstrate a settled frame feed should not do by default. Add
#
#     hud.BindFps(@view)
#
# and put "%f fps" in the text when you want the number.
eye.spawn(RenderProvider::TextLabel hud)
hud.Create(24)
# Says what this page can actually do, not what the control scheme is meant to
# be: the key edge is not attached here (see the pumps, below), so promising
# W/A/S/D on screen would be the caption lying about the build.
hud.SetText(pointer looks -- keys pending, see scene3d.etcs)
hud.SetPosition(16, 16)
hud.SetColor(0.6, 1.0, 0.7, 1.0)
hud.SetBackground(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.45)
hud.SetPadding(6)
hud.SetOrder(11)

eye.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D reticle)
reticle.Create()
reticle.AddPoint(506, 378)
reticle.AddPoint(518, 378)
reticle.AddPoint(518, 390)
reticle.AddPoint(506, 390)
reticle.SetFill(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.55)
reticle.SetOrder(10)

# ── the frame edge ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Compose, not Draw. Draw would issue one composition now and the surface would
# replay it forever -- right for a still picture, and exactly wrong for a tree
# that moves, since replaying past calls never asks the camera to make a new
# image.
view.Clear(0.02, 0.02, 0.03, 1.0)
view.Compose(@stage)

# ── the pumps ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A stream edge blocks the thread that states it, so everything but the last is
# detached -- and WHICH one is last is what the note at the top of this file is
# about. The FRAME edge is last, on this thread, because a projection on a
# detached thread corrupts the heap here; the POINTER edge is the one detached,
# because it demonstrably survives being.
#
# So this is not the arrangement scripts/scene3d.etcs uses, and the difference is
# a bug rather than a substrate. Once it is fixed, the frame edge goes back to
#
#     detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=view
#
# and the key half becomes the last edge, which is where W/A/S/D comes from:
#
#     main.ProduceEvents() -> world.ConsumeInput()
#
# Both lines are left here rather than quietly omitted, because a page that
# silently lacks half its controls is the kind of thing that gets rediscovered
# as a mystery.
detach scene3d_look.etcs window=main scene=world

# One tick, not a pair -- presenting is a step on the Presentable family now and
# the producer no longer stands on a pool worker. See render_frames.etcs.
view.RunFrames(16)
